WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.662
CARTER MORGAN: Welcome to Cloud
Security Basics, a new series

00:00:02.662 --> 00:00:04.590
where we explain the
ins and outs of securing

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.450
your application
on Google Cloud.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.170
Sound fun?

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.227
Then stick around
because in this episode,

00:00:09.227 --> 00:00:11.310
you'll learn about Google
Cloud's physical defense

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.458
of their data
centers and hardware.

00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:16.944
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.386 --> 00:00:33.203
BRAD ACTOR: Hello,
cloud detective.

00:00:33.203 --> 00:00:34.870
You may have solved
last week's question

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:36.650
about securing customers' data.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.590
But what about securing
the data centers

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.130
and other physical hardware
that runs your software?

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.780
You may have everything else
and your ecosystem is secure.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.730
What will you do about machine
or supply chain-related issues?

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.280
How can you be sure the
physical devices are

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.380
being properly secured?

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.010
Or what if some bad
actor plants malware

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.380
on your precious machines?

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.660
What can you do to stop that?

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.190
For your sake, I hope you know
the answer to my question.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.730
CARTER MORGAN: OK,
here's what we know.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.460
Physical security has
two components-- securing

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:13.410
the physical servers and the
data centers that data lives on

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.320
and ensuring the physical
and virtual systems

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.510
that applications
rely on are secure,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.580
like with a route of trust.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.280
If the physical devices
aren't being properly secured,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.260
someone could steal a
machine with your data

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.840
on it, plant malware
on it, or otherwise

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.880
tamper with the
machines, compromising

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.670
the security of anyone
who uses those instances.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.880
Luckily, Google Cloud provides
a lot of tools and services

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.190
to protect its users.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:39.950
When it comes to securing the
physical premises and hardware,

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:41.900
access to Google data
centers is limited

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.350
to a small number of specially
qualified Google employees.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.420
Less than 1% of
googlers will ever

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.093
set foot into a data center.

00:01:49.093 --> 00:01:50.510
Access to the data
center floor is

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.520
only possible via
security corridor, which

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:56.150
implements multi-factor access
controls using security badges

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.855
and biometrics.

00:01:57.855 --> 00:02:00.230
When it comes to securing the
virtualization and hardware

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.120
component software
runs on, Google

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.933
has designed a lot of custom
tooling to protect its users.

00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:06.350
Google data centers
have thousands

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.080
of server machines connected
to a local network providing

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.825
an initial layer of security.

00:02:10.825 --> 00:02:12.950
Both the server boards and
the networking equipment

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.770
are custom designed by Google to
adhere to their tough security

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:16.850
requirements.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.880
And Google's custom-designed
Titan hardware security chip

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.430
can be deployed on both
servers and peripherals,

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.800
allowing Google to identify
and authenticate devices

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.060
at the hardware level.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:27.740
With security like
that, you can be

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.505
sure the hardware your
software runs on is secure.

00:02:30.505 --> 00:02:31.880
Let's see a few
specific examples

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.280
of how Google
secures the platform.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.540
So how does Google Cloud
secure the data centers

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.992
and other physical hardware
that runs your software?

00:02:41.992 --> 00:02:47.560
Hmm, first, let's talk about
securing the data center.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.740
On top of the technologies,
like biometric identification,

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.140
metal detectors, camera,
physical barriers,

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.630
and laser-based
intrusion detection,

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:57.880
as you get closer to
the data center floor,

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.340
security measures also increase.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.960
Access to the data
center floor is

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.390
only possible via
security corridor, which

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.260
implements multi-factor access
controls using security badges

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.400
and biometrics.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.950
Plus, data centers are
routinely patrolled

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.470
by experienced
security guards who

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.580
have undergone rigorous
background checks and training.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.820
So that's the physical
defense of data centers.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:23.790
But what about virtual and
real hardware security?

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.520
Well, there's a Titan chip.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.170
Titan is a secure
low-power microcontroller

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:31.110
designed with Google security,
requirements, and hardware

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.990
scenarios in mind.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:35.110
Titan provides a hardware
based route of trust

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:38.080
that establishes a strong
identity for a machine, which

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.450
enables Google to make
important security decisions

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.200
and validate the
"health" of the system.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.580
Titan offers integrity
verification of firmware

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:47.670
and software components.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.240
The system's strong
identity ensures

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.670
that there's a true audit
trail of any changes done

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:52.960
to the system.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.610
Tamper-evident
logging capabilities

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:59.140
help identify actions performed
by an insider with root access.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:00.700
All of these features
come together

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.550
to allow Google to securely
run operating systems that

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.280
have been verified
and authenticated.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.400
And then there's KVM or the
kernel-based virtual machine

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.840
that Google uses as the
foundations of Google Compute

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.440
Engine and Google
Kubernetes engine.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.260
KVM is an open source
virtualization technology

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.540
built right into Linux
and lets you turn Linux

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.420
into a hypervisor that allows
one host machine to run

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.520
multiple isolated VMs.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.220
Plus, Google invests in
additional security hardening

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:29.830
and protections based on
his research and testing

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:30.800
experiences.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.220
For example, the
Google Security team

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.260
performs thorough
code reviews each time

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.230
they adopt a new feature
or version of KVM

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.900
and uses an extensive set
of proprietary fuzzing tools

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.390
for KVM to further test it.

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:47.130
Google removes unused KVM
components, like legacy mouse

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.170
drivers, to reduce
the attack service

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.630
for potential
adversaries to exploit.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:55.310
And instead of using the more
complex QEMU emulator built

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:58.290
into KVM, Google wrote
its own user space

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.900
virtual machine monitor and
hardware emulator, which

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.020
is simpler, better tested,
and offer significant security

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.290
advantages.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:06.750
These are just a
few of the steps

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.480
Google's taken to heart
in KVM for its services

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:10.830
and its customers.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.430
So we see how Google uses
hardware, virtualization,

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.774
and physical security
to protect its tooling.

00:05:17.774 --> 00:05:21.170
Mm, and I see how to answer
Brad Actor's question.

00:05:24.746 --> 00:05:27.180
BRAD ACTOR: I see you've
been busy, Detective.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.430
Somehow you answered
my little question.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.260
How do you secure the platform?

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.120
The data center's
another physical hardware

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.320
that runs your software.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.380
I see you found the
crux of the argument.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:39.780
Google's commitment to
security first design

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.110
keeps the platform secure.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.230
Google Cloud manages security
throughout the data lifecycle

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.870
from the data center to
the device using a range

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.330
of technologies and approaches.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.910
Very good-- very good.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:57.540
You seem to have the basics
covered, but be wary.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.900
I'll return with questions
about forensic and preventative

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.160
actions.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.050
You've been warned, Detective.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:05.466
Ha, ha, ha.

00:06:05.466 --> 00:06:07.270
CARTER MORGAN: So
there you have it.

